# DURABLE REPRO — a `Guard[T]` CAPTURED BY A CLOSURE does not compile: the
# closure environment is lowered as a struct, and the C backend emits
# `Guard__Inner g;` inside it without ever emitting the `Guard__Inner` typedef.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg):
#   `gg check`                -> "OK: no semantic errors"
#   `gg build`                -> C: error: unknown type name 'Guard__Inner'
#   `gg build --backend=llvm` -> the SAME C error in the emitted runtime .c
#
# ⚠ THE CAPTURE IS THE DISCRIMINATOR. The byte-identical program with the
# capture removed (`int() f = (): 7` plus a separate `print(g.fd)`) BUILDS AND
# RUNS CORRECTLY, printing `7` then `10` — measured at HEAD. So neither the
# guard local, nor the closure, nor the guard's field read is the defect; it is
# specifically a guard-typed value crossing into a closure environment.
#
# SCOPE — this fixture samples exactly ONE cell: `Guard[T]` at a CLOSURE-CAPTURE
# position. Its sibling `sound_guard_struct_field_cc_fail` samples the ordinary
# STRUCT-FIELD position and fails with the same diagnostic, which is what
# identifies the two as one class (the closure environment IS a struct).
# DISTINCT from `sound_guard_return_position_cc_fail` — that one emits the type
# fine and fails on a missing runtime symbol instead.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): a closure may capture a guard like any other value,
# and reading the guarded field through it prints `10`.
#
# ⚠ THIS FIXTURE CANNOT RUN UNTIL FIXED: no binary is produced on either lane.
#
# The seed is COMPUTED, not a literal, so no const-fold can elide the guard.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (guard/wrapper subsystem).
struct Inner:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

void main():
    Mutex[Inner] m = Mutex[Inner](Inner(seed()))
    Guard[Inner] g = m.lock()
    int() f = (): g.fd
    print(f())
